WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.290
A phone book holds a million names

2
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.890
in alphabetical order.

3
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.860
You want to find one of them.

4
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.928
Reading from the first page could

5
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.380
take a million looks.

6
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.900
But nobody does that.

7
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.560
You open the book in the middle

8
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.540
see whether your name comes before or after

9
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.410
and throw half the book away.

10
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.450
How many looks does that take?

11
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.480
At most twenty.

12
00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.420
Not twenty thousand.

13
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.720
Twenty.

14
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.789
Let's see why

15
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.176
and learn a tool for answering

16
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.790
this kind of question: the recurrence.

17
00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:34.900
This is binary search

18
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.450
and the first recurrence of the course.

19
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.101
We'll see how binary search works

20
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.071
how to turn it into a recurrence

21
00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.750
and solve it by unrolling

22
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.680
and how to read recurrences as trees.

23
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.870
Binary search looks at the middle item.

24
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.300
If it is the target, we are done.

25
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.900
If the target is smaller

26
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.160
we search the left half.

27
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.490
If it is larger, we search the right half.

28
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.866
The list must be sorted.

29
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.023
That is what lets a single look

30
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.200
rule out half of the items.

31
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.120
And notice that searching a half

32
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.140
is the same problem again, only smaller.

33
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.310
That is a recursive structure.

34
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.300
Pause and predict.

35
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.020
A list has a thousand items

36
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.650
and binary search needs at most ten looks.

37
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.890
Now the list doubles, to two thousand items.

38
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.360
How many looks, at most?

39
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:29.800
Ten more?

40
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.220
Five more?

41
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.590
Twice as many?

42
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.070
Just one more.

43
00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.137
Eleven.

44
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.789
One extra look cuts the doubled

45
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.020
list back to the old size.

46
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.450
Let T of n be the number of looks

47
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.320
in the worst case, for a list of n items.

48
00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.945
One look at the middle

49
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.796
and then the same problem on

50
00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.660
at most half of the list.

51
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.710
So T of n equals T of n over two, plus one.

52
00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.730
When one item is left, one look settles it.

53
00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.420
T of one is one.

54
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.766
To solve a recurrence, unroll it.

55
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.633
Replace T of n over two

56
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.520
by its own definition.

57
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:14.060
T of n is T of n over four, plus one

58
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:14.960
plus one.

59
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.590
Again: T of n over eight, plus three.

60
00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.270
After k steps

61
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.910
the problem has size n over two to the k

62
00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.340
and we have paid k looks.

63
00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.496
The list is down to one item

64
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.480
when two to the k equals n.

65
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.050
That is k equals log base two of n.

66
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.400
So T of n is log base two of n, plus one.

67
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.310
Let's check it against real runs.

68
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.450
Lists of size one, two, four, eight

69
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.730
sixteen need one, two, three, four

70
00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:49.900
five looks.

71
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.320
Every time the list doubles

72
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.250
we pay one more look.

73
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.950
A thousand items: ten looks.

74
00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.050
A million: twenty.

75
00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.440
A billion: just thirty.

76
00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.810
Many recursive algorithms fit one shape

77
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.025
a subproblems, each of size n over b

78
00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:11.191
plus some work to split the problem

79
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.860
and combine the answers.

80
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.240
Draw the recursion as a tree.

81
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.341
At depth k there are a to the k nodes

82
00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.986
and each one has a problem of

83
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.290
size n over b to the k.

84
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.770
The cost is the sum, level by level.

85
00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.290
So everything depends on one question

86
00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.070
as we go down

87
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.170
does the work per level shrink

88
00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.360
stay the same, or grow?

89
00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.160
If it stays the same

90
00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.589
every level costs the same

91
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.773
and the total is that cost times

92
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.460
the number of levels.

93
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.240
Binary search is like that

94
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.110
one look per level, log n levels.

95
00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.850
If the work shrinks as we go down

96
00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.570
the top level dominates.

97
00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.450
If it grows, the bottom level, the leaves

98
00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:54.580
dominates.

99
00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.460
This is the idea behind the master theorem.

100
00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.440
We will use the intuition

101
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.570
rather than memorize its cases.

102
00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.737
The same search can be written as a loop.

103
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.023
For a million items both need

104
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.030
the same twenty looks.

105
00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.514
The difference is the stack

106
00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.347
the recursive version has up

107
00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.570
to twenty frames waiting, the loop has none.

108
00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:18.820
Here that cost is tiny.

109
00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.980
To sum up: write the cost as a recurrence

110
00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.900
unroll it, and read it as a tree.

111
00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.240
Halving the problem gives a logarithm.

112
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.813
Next: merge sort

113
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.216
where we split in half but also have

114
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.170
to put the halves back together.

115
00:04:33.797 --> 00:04:35.847
Let's write binary search in Java

116
00:04:35.847 --> 00:04:37.237
and measure the looks.

117
00:04:37.817 --> 00:04:39.504
The public method takes the

118
00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:40.999
array and the target

119
00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:42.666
and hands the real work to a method

120
00:04:42.707 --> 00:04:45.227
with two extra parameters, lo and hi

121
00:04:45.227 --> 00:04:47.737
the bounds of the part still in play.

122
00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:51.537
If lo is greater than hi, nothing is left

123
00:04:51.537 --> 00:04:53.177
return minus one.

124
00:04:53.177 --> 00:04:55.417
Otherwise we look at the middle.

125
00:04:55.417 --> 00:04:58.037
Found: return the index.

126
00:04:58.037 --> 00:04:59.777
Smaller or larger

127
00:04:59.777 --> 00:05:02.287
search the right or the left half.

128
00:05:02.637 --> 00:05:06.027
Notice how the middle is computed: lo

129
00:05:06.027 --> 00:05:08.267
plus the difference hi minus lo

130
00:05:08.267 --> 00:05:09.867
divided by two.

131
00:05:09.867 --> 00:05:12.517
Not lo plus hi, divided by two.

132
00:05:13.097 --> 00:05:14.107
Why?

133
00:05:14.107 --> 00:05:17.045
In Java, an int holds at most about

134
00:05:17.127 --> 00:05:18.687
two point one billion.

135
00:05:18.687 --> 00:05:21.447
For two huge indexes

136
00:05:21.447 --> 00:05:24.413
lo plus hi overflows and goes negative

137
00:05:24.487 --> 00:05:26.467
and the program indexes the array

138
00:05:26.527 --> 00:05:28.097
at a negative position.

139
00:05:28.447 --> 00:05:31.696
This exact bug sat in the Java library's

140
00:05:31.777 --> 00:05:34.157
own binary search for years

141
00:05:34.157 --> 00:05:36.737
until it was fixed in two thousand six.

142
00:05:36.737 --> 00:05:38.297
Python cannot have it

143
00:05:38.297 --> 00:05:40.927
because its integers do not overflow.

144
00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.982
The static counter probes goes up

145
00:05:44.057 --> 00:05:46.377
once per look at a middle item.

146
00:05:46.377 --> 00:05:49.227
That's T of n from our recurrence.

147
00:05:49.807 --> 00:05:51.586
Here are the real counts.

148
00:05:51.657 --> 00:05:53.511
A size of two to the k needs

149
00:05:53.577 --> 00:05:55.517
k plus one looks.

150
00:05:55.517 --> 00:05:57.457
A thousand items: ten.

151
00:05:57.457 --> 00:05:59.377
A million: twenty.

152
00:05:59.377 --> 00:06:01.887
Exactly what the unrolled recurrence said.

153
00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:04.717
The loop version does the same thing

154
00:06:04.717 --> 00:06:06.597
moving lo and hi.

155
00:06:06.597 --> 00:06:10.027
Same twenty looks, but no stack frames.

156
00:06:10.607 --> 00:06:14.178
A tempting shortcut is to pass a copy of half

157
00:06:14.257 --> 00:06:16.217
of the array to the recursive call

158
00:06:16.217 --> 00:06:18.617
with Arrays dot copyOfRange.

159
00:06:18.617 --> 00:06:22.327
It reads nicely, but copying costs time.

160
00:06:22.677 --> 00:06:24.422
The first call alone copies

161
00:06:24.487 --> 00:06:25.889
half of the array.

162
00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:27.548
Over the whole search about

163
00:06:27.607 --> 00:06:29.407
n items are copied

164
00:06:29.407 --> 00:06:31.397
far more than twenty looks.

165
00:06:31.747 --> 00:06:34.747
Pass indexes, not copies.

166
00:06:35.327 --> 00:06:37.012
In practice you would call

167
00:06:37.077 --> 00:06:39.151
Arrays dot binarySearch.

168
00:06:39.237 --> 00:06:41.238
When the key is missing it returns

169
00:06:41.297 --> 00:06:43.790
minus the insertion point, minus one

170
00:06:43.857 --> 00:06:45.928
so negative results still tell

171
00:06:45.997 --> 00:06:47.687
you where the key would go.

172
00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:50.817
So in Java: pass bounds

173
00:06:50.817 --> 00:06:52.657
compute the midpoint safely

174
00:06:52.657 --> 00:06:55.747
and the counter confirms k plus one looks.

175
00:06:56.097 --> 00:07:00.657
Next: merge sort, our first n log n sort.

